course: dictionary
lesson: 4523
title: Headphones and sound
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Headphones are a pair of small speakers that you wear over or in your ears. They let you listen to music or sound without other people hearing it. The word is usually plural, so you say «a pair of headphones», but when it comes before another noun, like «headphone jack», it is singular.
explain: Context clues help you see how headphones are used. «Very loud music in headphones can damage hearing for good; a common tip is to keep the volume at about 60 percent and take breaks.» The clues are damage and hearing, so the volume matters for your ears. Always ask: what does this tell me about headphones?
explain: Some sentences teach a safety lesson. «A boy in Perth wore noise-cancelling headphones while walking behind a reversing truck; he did not hear its beeping and the driver stopped just in time.» He could not hear the warning, so you should not wear headphones when you need to hear traffic, such as when you cross a road or ride a bike.
fact: Headphones are a pair of small speakers worn over or in the ears.
fact: «Headphones» is usually plural, as in «a pair of headphones»; «headphone jack» uses it as a single word before another noun.
fact: «Noise-cancelling headphones» listen to noise and play the opposite wave, so they work best on steady droning noise, like a train or a plane engine.
fact: «Damage» means harm something so it is no longer good. Loud sound through headphones can damage your hearing.
fact: Keep the volume low, and take breaks, to protect your hearing. Do not wear headphones when you need to hear traffic.
exercise: «Very loud music in headphones can damage hearing for good.» What can very loud music in headphones do? | Make the music easier to hear for others | Turn the volume down by itself | Make your hearing stronger | *Damage your hearing for good
exercise: What is a pair of headphones? | A map of the sky | *A pair of small speakers worn over or in the ears | A microphone that records your voice | A tiny speaker that sits on a shelf
exercise: «A boy in Perth wore noise-cancelling headphones while walking behind a reversing truck.» What is the safety lesson? | Trucks are quiet, so headphones are always safe | Headphones make walking faster | *Do not wear headphones when you need to hear traffic | Wear headphones louder when you cross a road
test: «A microphone turns sound wobbles into electric signals, and a speaker turns signals back into sound.» According to the text, what does a speaker do? | *Turns electric signals back into sound | Turns sound into electric signals | Records voices for the internet | Stops loud noise
test: «A common tip is to keep the volume at about 60 percent and take breaks.» What is the tip for using headphones? | *Keep the volume at about 60 percent and take breaks | Turn the volume up when you cross the road | Wear them all day without a break | Keep the volume as loud as possible
exam: Which example shows a safe way to use headphones? | Wearing noise-cancelling headphones to cross a busy road | Turning the volume up to drown out the traffic | *Keeping the volume low and taking breaks | Wearing headphones while riding a bike
